  "name": "Question 38",
  "problem": "The curve $y=4{x}^{2}-24x+24$ can be written as $y=a(x+b)^{2}+c.$ Calculate $a+b+c.$",
  "worked_solution": "To express the equation of the parabola in vertex form, we need to complete the square. \n\nFirst, we take out a factor of $4$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-24x+24 & =4({x}^{2}-6x+6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-6x+6 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3\\cdot x+6 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3\\cdot x+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2}+6 & = \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3\\cdot x+{3}^{2})-{3}^{2}+6 & = \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-{3}^{2}+6 & = \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-9+6 & = \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}^{2}-6x+6) & = \\\\ 4((x-3)^{2}-3) & = \\\\ 4(x-3)^{2}-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,  $a+b+c=4+(-3)+(-12)=-11.$",
